   "evidence": "mathlib/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/CircleAverage.lean:62-66 `/-- If 'f' is *not* circle integrable, then the circle average is zero by definition. -/ theorem circleAverage.integral_undef`; Average.lean:64 defines `circleAverageTransverse` directly as `Real.circleAverage \u2026`.",
   "file": "EpsilonEridani/Particles/Parton/GTMD/Average.lean",
   "fix": "Add a sentence to the `circleAverageTransverse` docstring, e.g. \"Following Mathlib's convention, the value is `0` when `fun z => g (\u2026repr z)` is not circle integrable, so the identification with the mean requires circle integrability.\" Mirror it in the module docstring where `azimuthalAverage` is introduced as the bridge into `TMD.Tmd`.",
   "issue": "The docstring of `circleAverageTransverse` (and the matching `Main definitions` bullet, line 22) says it is \"the mean of `g` over the circle of radius `kT`\". That is false when `g` is not circle-integrable: `Real.circleAverage` is then `0` by definition. This convention is what lets `isTmdDensity_azimuthalAverage` (line 166) and `circleAverageTransverse_nonneg_of_nonneg` (line 108) hold with `IsVectorTmdDensity` carrying no measurability/integrability field, and nothing in the file's documentation tells the reader that.",
   "line": 62
  }
 ],
